          item-192 at level 5: text: 2
          item-193 at level 5: text: O emissions from storage manure and urine and dung deposits on pasture were also estimated using the IPCC [38] emission factors.
      item-194 at level 3: section_header: Farm management
        item-195 at level 4: text: Emissions due to farm management included those from fuel and machinery for manure handling and electricity for milking and confinement (Table 5). Emissions due to feed processing such as cutting, crushing, mixing and distributing, as well as secondary sources of emissions during the production of fuel, machinery, fertilizer, pesticides, seeds and plastic for ensilage were included in ‘Emissions from crop and pasture production’ section.
        item-196 at level 4: table with [12x4]
          item-196 at level 5: caption: Table 5 Factors for major resource inputs in farm management.
        item-197 at level 4: inline: group group
          item-198 at level 5: text: The amount of fuel use for manure handling were estimated taking into consideration the amount of manure produced per cow and the amounts of fuel required for manure handling (L diesel t
          item-199 at level 5: text: -1
          item-200 at level 5: text: ) [42]. The amount of manure was estimated from OM excretions (kg cow
          item-201 at level 5: text: -1
          item-202 at level 5: text: ), assuming that the manure has 8% ash on DM basis and 60% DM content. The OM excretions were calculated by NDOMI × days in confinement × proportion of daily time that animals stayed on confinement.
        item-203 at level 4: inline: group group
          item-204 at level 5: text: The emissions from fuel were estimated considering the primary (emissions from fuel burned) and secondary (emissions for producing and transporting fuel) emissions. The primary emissions were calculated by the amount of fuel required for manure handling (L) × (kg CO
