      item-231 at level 3: section_header: Co-product allocation
        item-232 at level 4: inline: group group
          item-233 at level 5: text: The C footprint for milk produced in the system was calculated using a biophysical allocation approach, as recommended by the International Dairy Federation [49], and described by Thoma et al. [48]. Briefly, AR
          item-234 at level 5: text: milk
          item-235 at level 5: text: = 1–6.04 × BMR, where
          item-236 at level 5: text: :
          item-237 at level 5: text: AR
          item-238 at level 5: text: milk
          item-239 at level 5: text: is the allocation ratio for milk and BMR is cow BW at the time of slaughter (kg) + calf BW sold (kg) divided by the total ECM produced during cow`s entire life (kg). The AR
          item-240 at level 5: text: milk
          item-241 at level 5: text: were 0.854 and 0.849 for TMR and TMR with both pasture scenarios, respectively. The AR
          item-242 at level 5: text: milk
          item-243 at level 5: text: was applied to the whole emissions, except for the electricity consumed for milking (milking parlor) and refrigerant loss, which was directly assigned to milk production.
      item-244 at level 3: section_header: Sensitivity analysis
        item-245 at level 4: text: A sensitivity index was calculated as described by Rotz et al. [42]. The sensitivity index was defined for each emission source as the percentage change in the C footprint for a 10% change in the given emission source divided by 10%. Thus, a value near 0 indicates a low sensitivity, whereas an index near or greater than 1 indicates a high sensitivity because a change in this value causes a similar change in the footprint.
    item-246 at level 2: section_header: Results and discussion
      item-247 at level 3: text: The study has assessed the impact of tropical and temperate pastures in dairy cows fed TMR on the C footprint of dairy production in subtropics. Different factors were taken in to consideration to estimate emissions from manure (or urine and dung) of grazing animals, feed production and electricity use.
      item-248 at level 3: section_header: Greenhouse gas emissions
        item-249 at level 4: inline: group group
          item-250 at level 5: text: Depending on emission factors used for calculating emissions from urine and dung (IPCC or local data) and feed production (Tables 3 or 4), the C footprint was similar (Fig 2A and 2B) or decreased by 0.04 kg CO
          item-251 at level 5: text: 2
          item-252 at level 5: text: e (kg ECM)
          item-253 at level 5: text: -1
          item-254 at level 5: text: (Fig 2C and 2D) in scenarios that included pastures compared to
          item-255 at level 5: text: ad libitum
          item-256 at level 5: text: TMR intake. Due to differences in emission factors, the overall GHG emission values ranged from 0.92 to 1.04 kg CO
          item-257 at level 5: text: 2
          item-258 at level 5: text: e (kg ECM)
          item-259 at level 5: text: -1
          item-260 at level 5: text: for dairy cows receiving TMR exclusively, and from 0.88 to 1.04 kg CO
          item-261 at level 5: text: 2
          item-262 at level 5: text: e (kg ECM)
          item-263 at level 5: text: -1
          item-264 at level 5: text: for cows with access to pasture. Using IPCC emission factors [38], manure emissions increased as TMR intake went down (Fig 2A and 2B). However, using local emission factors for estimating N
          item-265 at level 5: text: 2
          item-266 at level 5: text: O-N emissions [37], manure emissions decreased as TMR intake went down (Fig 2C and 2D). Regardless of emission factors used (Tables 3 or 4), emissions from feed production decreased to a small extent as the proportion of TMR intake decreased. Emissions from farm management did not contribute more than 5% of overall GHG emissions.
        item-267 at level 4: picture
          item-267 at level 5: caption: Fig 2 Overall greenhouse gas emissions in dairy cattle systems under various scenarios. TMR = ad libitum TMR intake, 75TMR = 75% of ad libitum TMR intake with access to pasture, 50TMR = 50% of ad libitum TMR intake with access to pasture. (a) N2O emission factors for urine and dung from IPCC [38], feed production emission factors from Table 3 without accounting for sequestered CO2-C from perennial pasture, production of electricity = 0.73 kg CO2e kWh-1 [41]. (b) N2O emission factors for urine and dung from IPCC [38], feed production emission factors from Table 3 without accounting for sequestered CO2-C from perennial pasture, production of electricity = 0.205 kg CO2e kWh-1 [46]; (c) N2O emission factors for urine and dung from local data [37], feed production EF from Table 4 without accounting for sequestered CO2-C from perennial pasture, production of electricity = 0.205 kg CO2e kWh-1 [46]. (d) N2O emission factors for urine and dung from local data [37], feed production emission factors from Table 4 accounting for sequestered CO2-C from perennial pasture, production of electricity = 0.205 kg CO2e kWh-1 [46].
        item-268 at level 4: inline: group group
          item-269 at level 5: text: Considering IPCC emission factors for N
          item-270 at level 5: text: 2
          item-271 at level 5: text: O emissions from urine and dung [38] and those from Table 3, the C footprint ranged from 0.99 to 1.04 kg CO
          item-272 at level 5: text: 2
          item-273 at level 5: text: e (kg ECM)
          item-274 at level 5: text: -1
          item-275 at level 5: text: , and was close to those reported under confined based systems in California [49], Canada [50], China [8], Ireland [9], different scenarios in Australia [51,52] and Uruguay [11], which ranged from 0.98 to 1.16 kg CO
          item-276 at level 5: text: 2
          item-277 at level 5: text: e (kg ECM)
          item-278 at level 5: text: -1
          item-279 at level 5: text: . When local emission factors for N
          item-280 at level 5: text: 2
          item-281 at level 5: text: O emissions from urine and dung [37] and those from Table 4 were taking into account, the C footprint for scenarios including pasture, without accounting for sequestered CO
          item-282 at level 5: text: 2
          item-283 at level 5: text: -C from perennial pasture—0.91 kg CO
          item-284 at level 5: text: 2
          item-285 at level 5: text: e (kg ECM)
          item-286 at level 5: text: -1
          item-287 at level 5: text: —was lower than the range of values described above. However, these values were still greater than high-performance confinement systems in UK and USA [53] or grass based dairy systems in Ireland [9,53] and New Zealand [8,54], which ranged from 0.52 to 0.89 kg CO
          item-288 at level 5: text: 2
          item-289 at level 5: text: e (kg ECM)
          item-290 at level 5: text: -1
          item-291 at level 5: text: . Regardless of which emission factor was used, we found a lower C footprint in all conditions compared to scenarios with lower milk production per cow or in poor conditions of manure management, which ranged from 1.4 to 2.3 kg CO
          item-292 at level 5: text: 2
          item-293 at level 5: text: e (kg ECM)
          item-294 at level 5: text: -1
          item-295 at level 5: text: [8,55]. Thus, even though differences between studies may be partially explained by various assumptions (e.g., emission factors, co-product allocation, methane emissions estimation, sequestered CO
          item-296 at level 5: text: 2
          item-297 at level 5: text: -C, etc.), herd productivity and manure management were systematically associated with the C footprint of the dairy systems.
        item-298 at level 4: inline: group group
          item-299 at level 5: text: The similarity of C footprint between different scenarios using IPCC [38] for estimating emissions from manure and for emissions from feed production (Table 3) was a consequence of the trade-off between greater manure emissions and lower emissions to produce feed, as the proportion of pasture in diets increased. Additionally, the small negative effect of pasture on ECM production also contributed to the trade-off. The impact of milk production on the C footprint was reported in a meta-analysis comprising 30 studies from 15 different countries [22]. As observed in this study (Fig 2A and 2B) the authors reported no significant difference between the C footprint of pasture-based vs. confinement systems. However, they observed that an increase of 1000 kg cow
          item-300 at level 5: text: -1
          item-301 at level 5: text: (5000 to 6000 kg ECM) reduced the C footprint by 0.12 kg CO
          item-302 at level 5: text: 2
          item-303 at level 5: text: e (kg ECM)
          item-304 at level 5: text: -1
          item-305 at level 5: text: , which may explain an apparent discrepancy between our study and an LCA performed in south Brazilian conditions [56]. Their study compared a confinement and a grazing-based dairy system with annual average milk production of 7667 and 5535 kg cow, respectively. In this study, the same herd was used in all systems, with an annual average milk production of around 7000 kg cow
          item-306 at level 5: text: -1
          item-307 at level 5: text: . Experimental data showed a reduction not greater than 3% of ECM when 50% of TMR was replaced by pasture access.
        item-308 at level 4: inline: group group
          item-309 at level 5: text: The lower C footprint in scenarios with access to pasture, when local emission factors [37] were used for N
          item-310 at level 5: text: 2
          item-311 at level 5: text: O emissions from urine and dung and for feed production (Table 4), may also be partially attributed to the small negative effect of pasture on ECM production. Nevertheless, local emission factors for urine and dung had a great impact on scenarios including pastures compared to
          item-312 at level 5: text: ad libitum
          item-313 at level 5: text: TMR intake. Whereas the IPCC [38] considers an emission of 0.02 kg N
          item-314 at level 5: text: 2
          item-315 at level 5: text: O-N (kg N)
          item-316 at level 5: text: -1
          item-317 at level 5: text: for urine and dung from grazing animals, experimental evidence shows that it may be up to five times lower, averaging 0.004 kg N
          item-318 at level 5: text: 2
          item-319 at level 5: text: O-N kg
          item-320 at level 5: text: -1
          item-321 at level 5: text: [37].
      item-322 at level 3: section_header: Methane emissions
        item-323 at level 4: inline: group group
          item-324 at level 5: text: The enteric CH
          item-325 at level 5: text: 4
          item-326 at level 5: text: intensity was similar between different scenarios (Fig 2), showing the greatest sensitivity index, with values ranging from 0.53 to 0.62, which indicate that for a 10% change in this source, the C footprint may change between 5.3 and 6.2% (Fig 3). The large effect of enteric CH
          item-327 at level 5: text: 4
          item-328 at level 5: text: emissions on the whole C footprint was expected, because the impact of enteric CH
          item-329 at level 5: text: 4
          item-330 at level 5: text: on GHG emissions of milk production in different dairy systems has been estimated to range from 44 to 60% of the total CO
          item-331 at level 5: text: 2
          item-332 at level 5: text: e [50,52,57,58]. However, emissions in feed production may be the most important source of GHG when emission factors for producing concentrate feeds are greater than 0.7 kg CO
          item-333 at level 5: text: 2
          item-334 at level 5: text: e kg
          item-335 at level 5: text: -1
          item-336 at level 5: text: [59], which did not happen in this study.
        item-337 at level 4: picture
          item-337 at level 5: caption: Fig 3 Sensitivity of the C footprint. Sensitivity index = percentage change in C footprint for a 10% change in the given emission source divided by 10% of. (a) N2O emission factors for urine and dung from IPCC [38], feed production emission factors from Table 3, production of electricity = 0.73 kg CO2e kWh-1 [41]. (b) N2O emission factors for urine and dung from IPCC [38], feed production emission factors from Table 3, production of electricity = 0.205 kg CO2e kWh-1 [46]; (c) N2O emission factors for urine and dung from local data [37], feed production EF from Table 4 without accounting sequestered CO2-C from perennial pasture, production of electricity = 0.205 kg CO2e kWh-1 [46]. (d) N2O emission factors for urine and dung from local data [37], feed production emission factors from Table 4 accounting sequestered CO2-C from perennial pasture, production of electricity = 0.205 kg CO2e kWh-1 [46].
        item-338 at level 4: inline: group group
          item-339 at level 5: text: The lack of difference in enteric CH
          item-340 at level 5: text: 4
          item-341 at level 5: text: emissions in different systems can be explained by the narrow range of NDF content in diets (<4% difference). This non-difference is due to the lower NDF content of annual temperate pastures (495 g (kg DM)
          item-342 at level 5: text: -1
          item-343 at level 5: text: ) compared to corn silage (550 g (kg DM)
          item-344 at level 5: text: -1
          item-345 at level 5: text: ). Hence, an expected, increase NDF content with decreased concentrate was partially offset by an increase in the pasture proportion relatively low in NDF. This is in agreement with studies conducted in southern Brazil, which have shown that the actual enteric CH
          item-346 at level 5: text: 4
          item-347 at level 5: text: emissions may decrease with inclusion of temperate pastures in cows receiving corn silage and soybean meal [60] or increase enteric CH
          item-348 at level 5: text: 4
          item-349 at level 5: text: emissions when dairy cows grazing a temperate pasture was supplemented with corn silage [61]. Additionally, enteric CH
          item-350 at level 5: text: 4
          item-351 at level 5: text: emissions did not differ between dairy cows receiving TMR exclusively or grazing a tropical pasture in the same scenarios as in this study [26].
      item-352 at level 3: section_header: Emissions from excreta and feed production
        item-353 at level 4: inline: group group
          item-354 at level 5: text: Using IPCC emission factors for N
          item-355 at level 5: text: 2
          item-356 at level 5: text: O emissions from urine and dung [38] and those from Table 3, CH
          item-357 at level 5: text: 4
          item-358 at level 5: text: emissions from manure decreased 0.07 kg CO
          item-359 at level 5: text: 2
          item-360 at level 5: text: e (kg ECM)
          item-361 at level 5: text: -1
          item-362 at level 5: text: , but N
          item-363 at level 5: text: 2
          item-364 at level 5: text: O emissions from manure increased 0.09 kg CO
          item-365 at level 5: text: 2
          item-366 at level 5: text: e (kg ECM)
          item-367 at level 5: text: -1
          item-368 at level 5: text: , as TMR intake was restricted to 50%
          item-369 at level 5: text: ad libitum
          item-370 at level 5: text: (Fig 4A). Emissions for pastures increased by 0.06 kg CO
          item-371 at level 5: text: 2
          item-372 at level 5: text: e (kg ECM)
          item-373 at level 5: text: -1
          item-374 at level 5: text: , whereas emissions for producing concentrate feeds and corn silage decreased by 0.09 kg CO
          item-375 at level 5: text: 2
          item-376 at level 5: text: e (kg ECM)
          item-377 at level 5: text: -1
          item-378 at level 5: text: , as TMR intake decreased (Fig 4B). In this situation, the lack of difference in calculated C footprints of different systems was also due to the greater emissions from manure, and offset by lower emissions from feed production with inclusion of pasture in lactating dairy cow diets. The greater N
          item-379 at level 5: text: 2
          item-380 at level 5: text: O-N emissions from manure with pasture was a consequence of higher N
          item-381 at level 5: text: 2
          item-382 at level 5: text: O-N emissions due to greater CP content and N urine excretion, as pasture intake increased. The effect of CP content on urine N excretion has been shown by several authors in lactating dairy cows [62–64]. For instance, by decreasing CP content from 185 to 152 g (kg DM)
          item-383 at level 5: text: -1
          item-384 at level 5: text: , N intake decreased by 20% and urine N excretion by 60% [62]. In this study, the CP content for lactating dairy cows ranged from 150 g (kg DM)
          item-385 at level 5: text: -1
          item-386 at level 5: text: on TMR system to 198 g (kg DM)
          item-387 at level 5: text: -1
          item-388 at level 5: text: on 50% TMR with pasture. Additionally, greater urine N excretion is expected with greater use of pasture. This occurs because protein utilization in pastures is inefficient, as the protein in fresh forages is highly degradable in the rumen and may not be captured by microbes [65].
        item-389 at level 4: picture
          item-389 at level 5: caption: Fig 4 Greenhouse gas emissions (GHG) from manure and feed production in dairy cattle systems. TMR = ad libitum TMR intake, 75TMR = 75% of ad libitum TMR intake with access to pasture, 50TMR = 50% of ad libitum TMR intake with access to pasture. (a) N2O emission factors for urine and dung from IPCC [38]. (b) Feed production emission factors from Table 3. (c) N2O emission factors for urine and dung from local data [37]. (d) Feed production emission factors from Table 4 accounting sequestered CO2-C from perennial pasture.
        item-390 at level 4: inline: group group
          item-391 at level 5: text: Using local emission factors for N
          item-392 at level 5: text: 2
          item-393 at level 5: text: O emissions from urine and dung [37] and those from Table 4, reductions in CH
          item-394 at level 5: text: 4
          item-395 at level 5: text: emissions from stocked manure, when pastures were included on diets, did not offset by increases in N
          item-396 at level 5: text: 2
          item-397 at level 5: text: O emissions from excreta (Fig 4C). In this case, total emissions from manure (Fig 4C) and feed production (Fig 4D) decreased with the inclusion of pasture. The impact of greater CP content and N urine excretion with increased pasture intake was offset by the much lower emission factors used for N
          item-398 at level 5: text: 2
          item-399 at level 5: text: O emissions from urine and dung. As suggested by other authors [66,67], these results show that IPCC default value may need to be revised for the subtropical region.
        item-400 at level 4: text: Emissions for feed production decreased when pasture was included due to the greater emission factor for corn grain production compared to pastures. Emissions from concentrate and silage had at least twice the sensitivity index compared to emissions from pastures. The amount of grain required per cow in a lifetime decreased from 7,300 kg to 4,000 kg when 50% of TMR was replaced by pasture access. These results are in agreement with other studies which found lower C footprint, as concentrate use is reduced and/or pasture is included [9,68,69]. Moreover, it has been demonstrated that in intensive dairy systems, after enteric fermentation, feed production is the second main contributor to C footprint [50]. There is potential to decrease the environmental impact of dairy systems by reducing the use of concentrate ingredients with high environmental impact, particularly in confinements [9].
      item-401 at level 3: section_header: Farm management
        item-402 at level 4: text: The lower impact of emissions from farm management is in agreement with other studies conducted in Europe [9, 62] and USA [42, 55], where the authors found that most emissions in dairy production systems are from enteric fermentation, feed production and emissions from excreta. As emissions from fuel for on-farm feed production were accounted into the ‘emissions from crop and pasture production’, total emissions from farm management were not greater than 5% of total C footprint.
        item-403 at level 4: inline: group group
          item-404 at level 5: text: Emissions from farm management dropped when the emission factor for electricity generation was based on the Brazilian matrix. In this case, the emission factor for electricity generation (0.205 kg CO
          item-405 at level 5: text: 2
          item-406 at level 5: text: e kWh
          item-407 at level 5: text: -1
          item-408 at level 5: text: [46]) is much lower than that in a LCA study conducted in US (0.73 kg CO
          item-409 at level 5: text: 2
          item-410 at level 5: text: e kWh
          item-411 at level 5: text: -1
          item-412 at level 5: text: [42]). This apparent discrepancy is explained because in 2016, almost 66% of the electricity generated in Brazil was from hydropower, which has an emission factor of 0.074 kg CO
          item-413 at level 5: text: 2
          item-414 at level 5: text: e kWh
          item-415 at level 5: text: -1
          item-416 at level 5: text: against 0.382 and 0.926 kg CO
          item-417 at level 5: text: 2
          item-418 at level 5: text: e kWh
          item-419 at level 5: text: -1
          item-420 at level 5: text: produced by natural gas and hard coal, respectively [46].
      item-421 at level 3: section_header: Assumptions and limitations
        item-422 at level 4: inline: group group
          item-423 at level 5: text: The milk production and composition data are the average for a typical herd, which might have great animal-to-animal variability. Likewise, DM yield of crops and pastures were collected from experimental observations, and may change as a function of inter-annual variation, climatic conditions, soil type, fertilization level etc. The emission factors for direct and indirect N
          item-424 at level 5: text: 2
          item-425 at level 5: text: O emissions from urine and dung were alternatively estimated using local data, but more experiments are necessary to reduce the uncertainty. The CO
          item-426 at level 5: text: 2
          item-427 at level 5: text: emitted from lime and urea application was estimated from IPCC default values, which may not represent emissions in subtropical conditions. This LCA may be improved by reducing the uncertainty of factors for estimating emissions from excreta and feed production, including the C sequestration or emissions as a function of soil management.
      item-428 at level 3: section_header: Further considerations
        item-429 at level 4: inline: group group
          item-430 at level 5: text: The potential for using pasture can reduce the C footprint because milk production kept pace with animal confinement. However, if milk production is to decrease with lower TMR intake and inclusion of pasture [19], the C footprint would be expected to increase. Lorenz et al. [22] showed that an increase in milk yield from 5,000 to 6,000 kg ECM reduced the C footprint by 0.12 kg CO
          item-431 at level 5: text: 2
          item-432 at level 5: text: e (kg ECM)
          item-433 at level 5: text: -1
          item-434 at level 5: text: , whereas an increase from 10,000 to 11,000 kg ECM reduced the C footprint by only 0.06 kg CO
          item-435 at level 5: text: 2
          item-436 at level 5: text: e (kg ECM)
          item-437 at level 5: text: -1
          item-438 at level 5: text: . Hence, the impact of increasing milk production on decreasing C footprint is not linear, and mitigation measures, such as breeding for increased genetic yield potential and increasing concentrate ratio in the diet, are potentially harmful for animal’s health and welfare [70]. For instance, increasing concentrate ratio potentially increases the occurrence of subclinical ketosis and foot lesions, and C footprint may increase by 0.03 kg CO
          item-439 at level 5: text: 2
          item-440 at level 5: text: e (kg ECM)
          item-441 at level 5: text: -1
          item-442 at level 5: text: in subclinical ketosis [71] and by 0.02 kg CO
          item-443 at level 5: text: 2
          item-444 at level 5: text: e (kg ECM)
          item-445 at level 5: text: -1
          item-446 at level 5: text: in case of foot lesions [72].
        item-447 at level 4: inline: group group
          item-448 at level 5: text: Grazing lands may also improve biodiversity [73]. Strategies such as zero tillage may increase stocks of soil C [74]. This study did not consider C sequestration during the growth of annual pastures, because it was assumed these grasses were planted with tillage, having a balance between C sequestration and C emissions [38]. Considering the C sequestration from no-tillage perennial pasture, the amount of C sequestration will more than compensates for C emitted. These results are in agreement with other authors who have shown that a reduction or elimination of soil tillage increases annual soil C sequestration in subtropical areas by 0.5 to 1.5 t ha
          item-449 at level 5: text: -1
          item-450 at level 5: text: [75]. If 50% of tilled areas were under perennial grasslands, 1.0 t C ha
          item-451 at level 5: text: -1
          item-452 at level 5: text: would be sequestered, further reducing the C footprint by 0.015 and 0.025 kg CO
          item-453 at level 5: text: 2
          item-454 at level 5: text: e (kg ECM)
          item-455 at level 5: text: -1
          item-456 at level 5: text: for the scenarios using 75 and 50% TMR, respectively. Eliminating tillage, the reduction on total GHG emissions would be 0.03 and 0.05 kg CO
          item-457 at level 5: text: 2
          item-458 at level 5: text: e (kg ECM)
          item-459 at level 5: text: -1
          item-460 at level 5: text: for 75 and 50% TMR, respectively. However, this approach may be controversial because lands which have been consistently managed for decades have approached steady state C storage, so that net exchange of CO
          item-461 at level 5: text: 2
          item-462 at level 5: text: would be negligible [76].
    item-463 at level 2: section_header: Conclusions
      item-464 at level 3: inline: group group
        item-465 at level 4: text: This study assessed the C footprint of dairy cattle systems with or without access to pastures. Including pastures showed potential to maintain or decrease to a small extent the C footprint, which may be attributable to the evidence of low N
        item-466 at level 4: text: 2
        item-467 at level 4: text: O emissions from urine and dung in dairy systems in subtropical areas. Even though the enteric CH
        item-468 at level 4: text: 4
        item-469 at level 4: text: intensity was the largest source of CO
        item-470 at level 4: text: 2
        item-471 at level 4: text: e emissions, it did not change between different scenarios due to the narrow range of NDF content in diets and maintaining the same milk production with or without access to pastures.
    item-472 at level 2: section_header: Acknowledgments
      item-473 at level 3: text: Thanks to Anna Naranjo for helpful comments throughout the elaboration of this manuscript, and to André Thaler Neto and Roberto Kappes for providing the key characteristics of the herd considered in this study.
